Scott's Theorem 7.2 states that if \(\mathbf{A}\), \(\mathbf{B}\), and \(\mathbf{C}\) are information systems then so is \(\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{B}\), that the approximable maps \(f:\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{B}\) are exactly the elements of \(|\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{B}|\), that there is an approximable \(\mathrm{apply}:(\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{C})\times\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{C}\) with \(\mathrm{apply}(g,y)=g(y)\), and that every approximable \(h:\mathbf{A}\times\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{C}\) has a unique \(\mathrm{curry}\,h:\mathbf{A}\to(\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{C})\) satisfying \(h=\mathrm{apply}\circ\langle(\mathrm{curry}\,h)\circ\mathrm{fst},\mathrm{snd}\rangle\), equivalently \(h(x,y)=(\mathrm{curry}\,h)(x)(y)\). `theorem_7_2` states only the two round trips `element_toApproxMap.approxMap_toElement` and `approxMap_toElement_element_toApproxMap`; `Theorem72` conjoins those with `applyMap_toElement` and with `curryMap_unique` together with `uncurry_curryMap`. `approxMap_toElement` sends a map to the element of function tokens whose input and output lie in the relation, taking consistency from `funCon_of_approxMap` and `approxMap_toElement.consistent` and deductive closure from `approxMap_toElement.closed`, whose step is the union lemma `rel_input_output_union` followed by monotonicity along the input entailments and then the output entailment. `element_toApproxMap` reads the relation back by membership of the packaged token `mkFunToken`, and the five approximable-map laws are `element_toApproxMap.rel_dom`, `element_toApproxMap.rel_cod`, `element_toApproxMap.empty_rel` (the bottom token, by Factoid 3.2), `element_toApproxMap.union_right`, and `element_toApproxMap.mono`. That is Scott's direction that an element, regarded as a binary relation, is approximable because the laws of Definition 5.1 are built into 7.1, and `funCon_of_approxMap` is the direction that a finite set of tokens lying on an approximable map satisfies the consistency clause 7.1(iii). Absent is Scott's generator \(\overline{w}=\{(u',v')\mid w\vdash(u',v')\}\) for finite consistent \(w\), and the criterion that \(w\vdash w'\) if and only if \(w'\subseteq\overline{w}\), if and only if every approximable \(f\) containing \(w\) contains \(w'\). The glosses treat the function-space system as already given by Definition 7.1 and do not prove, in this import list, that \(\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{B}\) satisfies the information-system axioms. `applyMap` relates a product-consistent set \(s\) to an output \(t\) when the function space entails, from the first component of \(s\), the function token whose input is the second component and whose output is \(t\), which is Scott's clause \(w\vdash(u',v')\) read on the two components of a product set. `applyMap_toElement` proves both inclusions of \(\mathrm{apply}(g,y)=g(y)\): a token of the image comes from a function token already in \(g\) whose input data lie in \(y\), and conversely that recovered token is placed in the product by `liftPair` of its singleton with its input, using `ProdCon_liftPair`, reflexive entailment of the input, and Proposition 2.3(iii). `curryMap` relates a consistent \(u\) to a set of function tokens when \(h\) relates `liftPair` of \(u\) with each token's input to that token's output, the shift from \((u,v)\mapsto w\) to \(u\mapsto(v,w)\), with codomain consistency from `funCon_of_curry_pointwise` and the union lemma `rel_of_funTokens`. `uncurryMap` is application after the pairing of the curried map following the first projection with the second projection, and `uncurry_curryMap` proves that \(h\) equals that composite in both directions by relation extensionality, using `entSet_liftPair` and the projection laws `fstMap_pairElements` and `sndMap_pairElements`. `curryMap_unique` shows this curry is the only map with that uncurrying, by extensionality at an element \(x\), evaluating both images at each \(y\) through `pairElements`, and cancelling by `applyMap_toElement` and the element round trip, which is the pointwise identity Scott uses for uniqueness. The later prose matches `constOp`, the curry of the first projection \(\mathbf{B}\times\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{B}\), so \(\mathrm{const}:\mathbf{B}\to(\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{B})\) and `constOp.toElement` sends \(b\) to the constant map with value \(b\); `pairOp`, from \((\mathbf{C}\to\mathbf{A})\times(\mathbf{C}\to\mathbf{B})\) into \(\mathbf{C}\to(\mathbf{A}\times\mathbf{B})\), with `pairOp.toElement` sending \((f,g)\) to \(\langle f,g\rangle\); and `compOp`, from \((\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{C})\times(\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{B})\) into \(\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{C}\), with `compOpUncurry.toElement` equal to \(g\) at the value of \(f\). The displayed isomorphism \(\mathbf{A}\times\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{C}\cong\mathbf{A}\to(\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{C})\) is that curry/uncurry equivalence; `tensorExpEquiv` is the same equivalence after a swap, as right curry `curryRight` from \(\mathbf{A}\otimes Y\to Z\) into \(Y\to(\mathbf{A}\to Z)\), and `tensorObj_eq_prod` with `ihom_obj_eq_exp` and `instCartesianMonoidalCategoryInfoSysObj` make the tensor the cartesian product and the internal hom the function space, which is the cartesian-closed structure the prose attributes to 6.2 and 7.2. Beyond this English, `conditional_iso` states that the conditional of elements \(x\) and \(y\) equals \(x\) at BOOL true and \(y\) at BOOL false and that every strict map out of BOOL is that conditional (`condMap.toElement_boolTrue`, `condMap.toElement_boolFalse`, `condMap.unique`), and `fixElement_fixed` with `fixMap` state Theorem 7.3's least-fixed-point equation and the approximable fixed-point operator; this note only says that the next result is an operator. **Theorem:** partial — the bijection, the apply equation, and the unique curry equation have the correct variance, but "\(\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{B}\) is an information system" is not stated. **Proof:** partial — the five relation laws, the component form of apply, and the curry/uncurry calculation match the written argument, while the generator \(\overline{w}\) and its entailment criterion are absent.
